<header><p class="st-kicker">margraviate · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Margraviate of Lower Lusatia</h1><p class="st-meta">965 CE – 1815 CE (approx.) · capital Lübben</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The march of Lusatia between the Elbe and the Oder, a homeland of the Sorbs passed between Germany, Poland and Bohemia before Prussia took it in 1815.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gero-the-great">Gero</a> died in 965, Otto I made the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/marca-geronis">Saxon Eastern March</a>, later called the March of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusatia">Lusatia</a>, a separate margraviate. In 1002 the Polish ruler Bolesław I seized it, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> held it until 1031. From 1136 it was held mainly by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wettin">Wettin</a> margraves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The land around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubben">Lübben</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cottbus">Cottbus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guben">Guben</a> was largely rural, with a strong Sorbian-speaking population. Lordship changed often: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> held it from 1304, and in 1367 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iv">Emperor Charles IV</a> bought it for the Bohemian crown. The estates of the land kept substantial self-government under these distant rulers.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-prague-1635">Peace of Prague</a> of 1635 transferred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-lusatia">Lower Lusatia</a> from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Bohemia</a> to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/electorate-of-saxony">Electorate of Saxony</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> gave it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1815, and it became part of the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>. The Lower Sorbian language is still spoken around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cottbus">Cottbus</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/margraviate-of-lower-lusatia.jpg" alt="Margraviate of Lower Lusatia" width="320" height="404"><figcaption>Image: Hugo Gerhard Ströhl (1851-1919) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Niederlausitz_Wappen.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>965 CE – 1815 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>margraviate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lübben</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gero II, Dietrich of Wettin</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity, later Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German, Lower Sorbian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>, <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>March of Lusatia, Niederlausitz</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1390&amp;focus=margraviate-of-lower-lusatia" aria-label="Margraviate of Lower Lusatia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5007 388 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Margraviate of Lower Lusatia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5327 644L5229 694M5327 644L5330 692M5327 644L5312 664M5327 644L5300 622"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5330 692h0M5312 664h0M5300 622h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5327 644h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5327 644h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1390&amp;focus=margraviate-of-lower-lusatia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
